                     <font>
                        <Font size="28.0" />
                     </font>
                  </Label>
                  <Label maxWidth="1.7976931348623157E308" text="&quot;The game is played with two teams. Each team elects a spymaster for the round.The grid of 25 words in front of us represent the codenames of the spies in the field. Some of these codenames correspond to your team's friendly spies, some are the other team's spies, some are innocent bystanders, and one is even a deadly assassin.The spymasters have access to a secret index which tells them what each codename corresponds to. (Show the spymaster card). Their goal is to covertly communicate the identity of their agents to the rest of the team members. The game ends once one team has identified all of their agents. The spymasters will alternate turns trying to accomplish this.In order to share this info, the spymaster must utter a clue with a specific format: a single word followed by a number. The word is a semantic link or theme that links together some of the codenames of your team's agents -- the number tells you how many codenames fall under that hint. For instance, one might say 'Apple, 3' to associate 'Pie', 'Tree' and 'Food'. (Use an example of something on the board laid out).Once the spymaster has provided a clue, the rest of their team attempts to guess the codenames one at a time. You're encouraged to discuss these out loud. Once you've settled on a guess, one of your team members will tap that card to signify their selection. The other team's spymaster will then use one of these tokens to indicate the identity of that codename. If you guess incorrectly, your team's turn ends. Picking an innocent bystander doesn't hurt you; picking one of other team's agents brings them closer to victory; and picking the assassin immediately loses the game for your team, so be careful. If you guessed correctly, you may keep guessing, up to the number provided in the clue plus one. You get that extra guess in case your team missed part of a clue from an earlier turn -- that way you can catch up.(If you haven't already, figure out your spymasters). Some extra info on being a spymaster: remember that the clue has to be single word. You can't say any word that's on the grid, and that includes parts of compound words like 'horse' in 'horseshoe'. And again, the clues need to relate the meaning of the codenames -- so you can't say something like 'S, 4' to link together 'shoe', 'string', 'sandwich' and 'Switzerland'. Finally, you can't say anything while your team is guessing -- try your best to keep a straight face so you don't accidentally give away any info. (Having the teams tap their guesses, and having the other spymaster do the scoring helps prevent body language cues)Any questions? Great, let's play!&quot;Things you want to be clear on:Make sure the spymasters know what's okay to say.Make sure the spymasters can interpret the index.Remind them about how the extra guess works once it's relevant. (For this reason, I don't recommend being spymaster in the first round)el" wrapText="true">
